  "decisions": [
    {
      "question": "Where does the org's weight insist?",
      "answer": "In the default view (units, activities, people, stakeholders, commitments) the heaviest nodes are the two stakeholder types — enterprise-clients and mid-market-clients, each connected to 13 other things. Every client-facing activity is involved with at least one of them, and the two cardinal commitments are for them. The three discipline units (design, strategy, digital-product) cluster at degree 7 each; client-services and operations sit just below at 6 — the first because it coordinates engagements rather than running them, the second because it does not produce client work at all.\n\nFor the leader: the topology mirrors the studio's positioning — three production disciplines, a coordination function, and a back-office, all of it pointing at the same two stakeholder types. If the studio rebalanced toward a third stakeholder type — strategic-partners, which today is a floating node with no incoming relations — the picture would look different; right now every activity reaches the same two endpoints.",
      "source": "nodes/stakeholders/enterprise-clients.md, nodes/stakeholders/mid-market-clients.md, commitments/studio-mid-market-baseline.md",
      "node_ids": [
        "enterprise-clients",
        "mid-market-clients",
        "design",
        "strategy",
        "digital-product",
        "client-services",
        "operations",
        "strategic-partners"
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "Which commitment carries the most structural weight?",
      "answer": "studio-enterprise-baseline binds five units (strategy, design, digital-product, client-services, operations) and is for enterprise-clients — six relations in one node, more than any other commitment. studio-mid-market-baseline carries the same shape on the mid-market side at slightly lower density: it binds the four client-facing units but does not pull operations in, because mid-market engagements are simpler and don't require the embedded-team coordination that operations provides for enterprise work. The two internal commitments — studio-employees-development binds four units to a development practice, studio-vendors-payments binds operations to vendors only — sit on their own with a much smaller blast radius. They are real but local.\n\nFor the leader: the enterprise and mid-market commitments are the load-bearing promises of the studio. If either one fails the picture loses its centre. The vendor-payments and employee-development commitments are structurally sound but they bind fewer parties, so a change there has narrower consequences. Worth treating the two big commitments as covenant artefacts — formally reviewed, formally renewed — and the other two as policy artefacts maintained at lower frequency.",
      "source": "commitments/studio-enterprise-baseline.md, commitments/studio-mid-market-baseline.md, commitments/studio-vendors-payments.md, commitments/studio-employees-development.md",
      "node_ids": [
