    {
      "question": "Where is the structure written most thinly?",
      "answer": "Three places stand out in the default view. First, strategic-partners is a floating node with no incoming relations — no commitment names it as a party, no activity involves it. The label exists in the structure but the relationship has not been written down: either the studio does work with strategic partners that has not been documented yet, or the term is aspirational and the floating node is the honest signal. Second, studio-vendors has only two default-view connections — vendor-invoicing involves it, studio-vendors-payments is for it — and nothing else: vendor relationships exist as a financial fact more than as an operational presence. Third, quarterly-finance-review is the lowest-degree activity in the graph (2 connections, half the median for activities); it is real and necessary but it involves nothing client-side.\n\nThe conditional reading: thin does not mean missing. It means the structure has not been written down here yet, or the part that exists is genuinely narrow. The two big-shape commitments are where the studio insists; the thin nodes are where it does not.",
